      "text": "Given that \\(10, a, b, ab\\) is an arithmetic progression, we know that the difference between consecutive terms is constant. Therefore, we can write the following equations based on the definition of an arithmetic progression:\n\n\\[\na - 10 = b - a \\quad \\text{(1)}\n\\]\n\\[\nb - a = ab - b \\quad \\text{(2)}\n\\]\n\nFirst, solve equation (1) for \\(b\\):\n\n\\[\na - 10 = b - a \\implies 2a - 10 = b \\implies b = 2a - 10\n\\]\n\nNext, substitute \\(b = 2a - 10\\) into equation (2):\n\n\\[\n(2a - 10) - a = a(2a - 10) - (2a - 10)\n\\]\n\\[\na - 10 = 2a^2 - 10a - 2a + 10\n\\]\n\\[\na - 10 = 2a^2 - 12a + 10\n\\]\n\\[\n0 = 2a^2 - 13a + 20\n\\]\n\nNow, solve the quadratic equation \\(2a^2 - 13a + 20 = 0\\) using the quadratic formula \\(a = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\), where \\(a = 2\\), \\(b = -13\\), and \\(c = 20\\):\n\n\\[\na = \\frac{13 \\pm \\sqrt{169 - 160}}{4} = \\frac{13 \\pm \\sqrt{9}}{4} = \\frac{13 \\pm 3}{4}\n\\]\n\nThis gives us two solutions for \\(a\\):\n\n\\[\na = \\frac{16}{4} = 4 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad a = \\frac{10}{4} = \\frac{5}{2}\n\\]\n\nWe now find the corresponding values of \\(b\\) for each \\(a\\):\n\n1. If \\(a = 4\\), then \\(b = 2(4) - 10 = 8 - 10 = -2\\).\n2. If \\(a = \\frac{5}{2}\\), then \\(b = 2\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right) - 10 = 5 - 10 = -5\\).\n\nThus, the pairs \\((a, b)\\) are \\((4, -2)\\) and \\(\\left(\\frac{5}{2}, -5\\right)\\). There are no other solutions.\n\nTherefore, the number of pairs \\((a, b)\\) is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
