      "text": "We are given that the sequence \\(10, a, b, ab\\) is an arithmetic progression. In an arithmetic progression, the difference between consecutive terms is constant. Let's denote this common difference by \\(d\\). Therefore, we can express the terms in the sequence as follows:\n\n1. The first term is \\(10\\).\n2. The second term is \\(a\\), so the common difference \\(d\\) between the first and second terms is \\(a - 10 = d\\).\n3. The third term is \\(b\\), so the common difference \\(d\\) between the second and third terms is \\(b - a = d\\).\n4. The fourth term is \\(ab\\), so the common difference \\(d\\) between the third and fourth terms is \\(ab - b = d\\).\n\nSince \\(d\\) is the same for all consecutive terms, we can set up the following equations:\n\n\\[\na - 10 = d\n\\]\n\\[\nb - a = d\n\\]\n\\[\nab - b = d\n\\]\n\nFrom the first equation, we have \\(d = a - 10\\). Substituting \\(d\\) into the second equation, we get:\n\n\\[\nb - a = a - 10 \\implies b = 2a - 10\n\\]\n\nNow substitute \\(d = a - 10\\) into the third equation:\n\n\\[\nab - b = a - 10 \\implies b(a - 1) = a - 10\n\\]\n\nSince \\(b \\neq 0\\) (otherwise all terms would be zero, which is not a meaningful arithmetic progression in this context), we can divide both sides by \\(b - 1\\):\n\n\\[\na - 1 = \\frac{a - 10}{b - 1}\n\\]\n\nWe also have \\(b = 2a - 10\\). Substitute \\(b = 2a - 10\\) into the equation:\n\n\\[\na - 1 = \\frac{a - 10}{2a - 11} \\implies (a - 1)(2a - 11) = a - 10\n\\]\n\nExpanding and simplifying:\n\n\\[\n2a^2 - 11a - 2a + 11 = a - 10 \\implies 2a^2 - 13a + 21 = a - 10 \\implies 2a^2 - 14a + 31 = 0\n\\]\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\(a = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\), where \\(a = 2\\), \\(b = -14\\), and \\(c = 31\\):\n\n\\[\na = \\frac{14 \\pm \\sqrt{196 - 248}}{4} = \\frac{14 \\pm \\sqrt{-52}}{4}\n\\]\n\nSince the discriminant \\(\\sqrt{-52}\\) is negative, there are no real solutions for \\(a\\). Therefore, 